-- 1. [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · Skill 2 · ans B] id 54ca0064-f1c7-4efb-9659-27783e9e7702
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A mixture of free histidine (\(\text{p}K_a\): \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\approx 1.8\), imidazole \(\approx 6.0\), \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\approx 9.2\)) and free aspartate (\(\text{p}K_a\): \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\approx 2.0\), \(\beta\text{-COOH}\approx 3.9\), \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\approx 9.9\)) is loaded at the center of an electrophoresis gel buffered at pH 6.0. After the field is applied, which outcome most likely describes how the two amino acids separate?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"Both species migrate toward the anode, because at pH 6.0 each amino acid lies above its respective \\(\\text{pI}\\) and carries a net negative charge."},{"label":"B","text":"Histidine moves toward the cathode and aspartate toward the anode, with aspartate migrating the farther of the two because its net charge is larger in magnitude."},{"label":"C","text":"Histidine moves toward the cathode and aspartate toward the anode, with histidine migrating the farther of the two because it is the more basic residue."},{"label":"D","text":"Aspartate migrates toward the anode while histidine stays at the origin, because at pH 6.0 histidine is at its \\(\\text{pI}\\) and carries zero net charge."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is B because the separation is governed by two independent facts that must be chained: the sign of each net charge (which sets the direction of migration) and the magnitude of each net charge (which sets how far each migrates in the field). First fix the \(\text{pI}\) of each amino acid using the average of the two \(\text{p}K_a\) values that flank its neutral (zero-charge) form. For histidine the neutral species sits between the imidazole and \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) groups, so \(\text{pI}=(6.0+9.2)/2=7.6\); the gel pH of 6.0 lies below this \(\text{pI}\), so histidine is a net cation and migrates toward the cathode (the negative electrode). For aspartate the neutral form sits between the two carboxyls, so \(\text{pI}=(2.0+3.9)/2=2.95\); pH 6.0 lies above this \(\text{pI}\), so aspartate is a net anion and migrates toward the anode. The two therefore move toward opposite electrodes. Now compare magnitudes group-by-group at pH 6.0. Histidine: \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) (\(\text{p}K_a 1.8\)) is deprotonated (\(-1\)); imidazole (\(\text{p}K_a 6.0\)) is exactly half protonated, contributing about \(+0.5\); \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) (\(\text{p}K_a 9.2\)) stays protonated (\(+1\)); the average net charge is about \(-1+0.5+1=+0.5\). Aspartate: both carboxyls (\(\text{p}K_a 2.0\) and \(3.9\)) are deprotonated (\(-1\) and \(\approx -1\)) and \(\alpha\text{-}\text{NH}_3^+\) (\(\text{p}K_a 9.9\)) stays protonated (\(+1\)), giving about \(-1-1+1=-1\). Because electrophoretic mobility scales with the magnitude of net charge, aspartate (\(|{-1}|\)) migrates farther than histidine (\(|{+0.5}|\)). (Choice A) This treats both residues as anions, which would require pH 6.0 to lie above both \(\text{pI}\) values; but pH 6.0 is below histidine's \(\text{pI}\) of 7.6, so histidine is a net cation and moves toward the cathode, not the anode. (Choice C) This assigns the correct migration directions but inverts the magnitude comparison: histidine's net charge of about \(+0.5\) is smaller than aspartate's \(-1\), so histidine migrates less, not more; being the more basic residue does not by itself confer greater mobility, since mobility tracks the magnitude of net charge at the operating pH. (Choice D) This mistakes histidine's imidazole \(\text{p}K_a\) of 6.0 for its \(\text{pI}\); a single \(\text{p}K_a\) equal to the buffer pH means that one group is half-titrated, not that the whole molecule is neutral. Histidine's \(\text{pI}\) is actually 7.6, so at pH 6.0 it carries a net charge near \(+0.5\) and does migrate toward the cathode rather than remaining at the origin. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the pH-versus-\(\text{pI}\) rule for direction with a group-by-group net-charge calculation for magnitude to rank the migration of two different amino acids.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '54ca0064-f1c7-4efb-9659-27783e9e7702';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '54ca0064-f1c7-4efb-9659-27783e9e7702';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('54ca0064-f1c7-4efb-9659-27783e9e7702', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Assumes both amino acids are anions at pH 6.0 without checking each pI separately, reversing the sign of histidine's net charge (pH 6.0 is below His pI 7.6, so His is cationic).$a$),
  ('54ca0064-f1c7-4efb-9659-27783e9e7702', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Gets both migration directions right but reverses the magnitude comparison, equating 'more basic residue' with greater mobility instead of ranking by the magnitude of net charge (His +0.5 < Asp -1).$a$),
  ('54ca0064-f1c7-4efb-9659-27783e9e7702', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Confuses a single group's pKa equal to the buffer pH (imidazole pKa 6.0, half-titrated) with the molecule's isoelectric point, concluding His is neutral when its true pI is 7.6 and its net charge is about +0.5.$a$);

-- 2. [Amino Acid Titration & Isoelectric Point · Skill 4 · ans D] id 5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A solution of free histidine is buffered so that the imidazole side chain (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 6.0\)) sits exactly at its half-equivalence point, where it is 50% protonated. Using histidine's other group \(\text{pK}_a\) values — \(\alpha\)-carboxyl \(\approx 1.8\) and \(\alpha\)-amino \(\approx 9.2\) — what average net charge does histidine most likely carry under these conditions?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"A net charge of \\(0\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"A net charge of \\(+1.5\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"A net charge of \\(+1\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"A net charge of \\(+0.5\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is D because the half-equivalence condition fixes the solution pH at the imidazole \(\text{pK}_a\), and summing the fractional charges at that pH gives an average net charge of \(+0.5\). First, identify the controlling constraint: a group is exactly 50% protonated only when \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_a\), so stating that the imidazole is at its half-equivalence point fixes \(\text{pH} = 6.0\). Next, assign each group at \(\text{pH} = 6.0\) by comparing pH to its \(\text{pK}_a\). The \(\alpha\)-carboxyl (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 1.8\)) has \(\text{pH} 6.0 \gg 1.8\), so it is essentially fully deprotonated to \(\text{COO}^-\), contributing \(-1\). The \(\alpha\)-amino group (\(\text{pK}_a \approx 9.2\)) has \(\text{pH} 6.0 \ll 9.2\), so it is essentially fully protonated as \(\text{NH}_3^+\), contributing \(+1\). The imidazole side chain is exactly half-protonated, so on average half the population carries the \(+1\) imidazolium charge and half is neutral, contributing \(+0.5\). Summing the average contributions gives \((-1) + (+1) + (+0.5) = +0.5\). This is consistent with the isoelectric point \(\text{pI} = (6.0 + 9.2)/2 = 7.6\): because the solution pH of \(6.0\) lies below \(\text{pI}\), histidine must be net positive, and the fractional value reflects that the side chain is only partially titrated here. (Choice A) A net charge of \(0\) results from treating histidine as a simple zwitterion bearing only \(\text{COO}^-\) and \(\text{NH}_3^+\) and ignoring the ionizable imidazole side chain; that side chain is half-protonated at \(\text{pH} 6.0\) and adds \(+0.5\), so the true average is not zero. (Choice B) A net charge of \(+1.5\) keeps the imidazole contribution at \(+0.5\) and the amino at \(+1\) but fails to deprotonate the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl; since \(\text{pH} 6.0\) is far above the carboxyl \(\text{pK}_a\) of \(1.8\), that group is \(\text{COO}^-\) and supplies \(-1\), lowering the total from \(+1.5\) to \(+0.5\). (Choice C) A net charge of \(+1\) is obtained by rounding the half-protonated imidazole up to a full \(+1\) instead of recognizing that a 50% population average contributes only \(+0.5\); whole-number charge assignment is valid only when a group lies well above or well below its \(\text{pK}_a\), not when \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_a\). This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must convert a stated half-equivalence condition into a numerical pH, evaluate the fractional ionization state of three groups, and combine those partial charges into a single average value.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Treats the amino acid as a simple zwitterion and ignores the ionizable imidazole side chain, dropping its +0.5 contribution$a$),
  ('5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133', 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Fails to deprotonate the carboxyl group at pH 6.0 (omits the -1 from COO-), keeping the running sum 1.0 unit too high$a$),
  ('5f96036d-c259-4ded-9c8f-7f2f837ed133', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Rounds the half-protonated (50%) imidazole up to a whole +1 charge instead of averaging the population to +0.5$a$);

-- 3. [Autoionization, Kw & the pH/pOH Scales · Skill 4 · ans A] id 9f3f135e-1f03-4284-91a9-47bc51d7170e
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist dilutes \(25.0\ \text{mL}\) of \(0.040\ \text{M}\ \text{NaOH}\) with pure water to a final volume of \(100.0\ \text{mL}\) and holds the resulting solution at \(40^\circ\text{C}\), where the autoionization of water gives \(K_w = 2.9\times10^{-14}\) (\(\text{p}K_w = 13.54\)). What is the pH of the diluted solution at this temperature?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 11.54\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 12.00\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 12.14\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 2.00\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because the diluted hydroxide concentration must first be found and then carried through the temperature-specific autoionization relationship. NaOH is a strong base that dissociates completely, so the moles of \(\text{OH}^-\) are conserved on dilution: \(n_{\text{OH}^-} = (0.0250\ \text{L})(0.040\ \text{M}) = 1.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\). Diluting to \(0.100\ \text{L}\) gives \([\text{OH}^-] = (1.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol})/(0.100\ \text{L}) = 1.0\times10^{-2}\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pOH} = -\log(1.0\times10^{-2}) = 2.00\). At \(40^\circ\text{C}\) the controlling constraint is that \(\text{p}K_w = 13.54\), not 14.00, so \(\text{pH} + \text{pOH} = \text{p}K_w = 13.54\) and \(\text{pH} = 13.54 - 2.00 = 11.54\). Equivalently, \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = K_w/[\text{OH}^-] = (2.9\times10^{-14})/(1.0\times10^{-2}) = 2.9\times10^{-12}\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pH} = -\log(2.9\times10^{-12}) = 11.54\). (Choice B) A value of 12.00 results from applying the \(25^\circ\text{C}\) scale, \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - \text{pOH} = 14.00 - 2.00\), which uses \(\text{p}K_w = 14.00\) and ignores that at \(40^\circ\text{C}\) the larger \(K_w\) lowers \(\text{p}K_w\) to 13.54, raising \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and lowering the pH below 12. (Choice C) A value of 12.14 results from omitting the dilution step and using the original \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.040\ \text{M}\) with the correct \(\text{p}K_w\): \(\text{pOH} = -\log(0.040) = 1.40\) gives \(\text{pH} = 13.54 - 1.40 = 12.14\); this fails to account for the fourfold dilution from \(25.0\ \text{mL}\) to \(100.0\ \text{mL}\), which lowers \([\text{OH}^-]\) to \(0.010\ \text{M}\). (Choice D) A value of 2.00 is the pOH of the diluted solution, \(-\log[\text{OH}^-]\), reported directly as the pH without converting through \(K_w\); a strongly basic NaOH solution cannot have a pH of 2.00. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must extract the conserved quantity through a dilution, propagate that intermediate concentration into a temperature-shifted ion-product relationship, and combine the two stages to reach the pH.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '9f3f135e-1f03-4284-91a9-47bc51d7170e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9f3f135e-1f03-4284-91a9-47bc51d7170e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9f3f135e-1f03-4284-91a9-47bc51d7170e', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Applies the 25 C relationship pH + pOH = 14.00 instead of using the given pKw = 13.54, dropping the temperature constraint that governs which ion-product value applies.$a$),
  ('9f3f135e-1f03-4284-91a9-47bc51d7170e', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Omits the dilution step and uses the original 0.040 M hydroxide concentration with the correct pKw, skipping the conserved-moles calculation that yields the diluted 0.010 M value.$a$),
  ('9f3f135e-1f03-4284-91a9-47bc51d7170e', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Stops after computing pOH = 2.00 and reports it as the pH, omitting the Kw/pKw conversion that links pOH to pH.$a$);

-- 4. [Autoionization, Kw & the pH/pOH Scales · Skill 4 · ans D] id b01d331b-8243-4743-a606-4ab6839b6318
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$At \(60\,^\circ\text{C}\) the autoionization of water gives \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-13}\). An aqueous sample held at this temperature is measured to have \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 2.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\). The \(\text{pOH}\) of this sample and its acid-base character are most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pOH} = 6.70\\); basic"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pOH} = 6.30\\); acidic"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pOH} = 7.30\\); acidic"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pOH} = 6.30\\); basic"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the analysis must use the temperature-specific \(K_w\) at every step. First, rearranging \(K_w = [\text{H}_3\text{O}^+][\text{OH}^-]\) gives \([\text{OH}^-] = K_w/[\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = (1.0\times10^{-13})/(2.0\times10^{-7}) = 0.50\times10^{-6} = 5.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pOH} = -\log(5.0\times10^{-7}) = 7 - \log 5.0 = 7 - 0.70 = 6.30\). Second, the neutral point at \(60\,^\circ\text{C}\) is not \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 1.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\) but \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = [\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{K_w} = \sqrt{1.0\times10^{-13}} \approx 3.2\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\). Because the sample's \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 2.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\) lies below this neutral value (equivalently \([\text{OH}^-] = 5.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M} > [\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 2.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\)), the solution is basic. (Choice A) A value of \(6.70\) is the \(\text{pH}\) of the sample, \(-\log(2.0\times10^{-7}) = 6.70\), mistakenly reported in place of the \(\text{pOH}\); the basic classification is correct, but \(\text{pH}\) and \(\text{pOH}\) have been interchanged. (Choice B) The \(\text{pOH}\) of \(6.30\) is computed correctly, but the classification compares \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 2.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\) against the \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\) neutral value of \(1.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\); since the temperature-shifted neutral concentration is \(3.2\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\), applying the wrong reference point reverses the true relationship in which \([\text{OH}^-]\) exceeds \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\). (Choice C) A \(\text{pOH}\) of \(7.30\) arises from using \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\) (the \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\) value) to obtain \([\text{OH}^-] = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(2.0\times10^{-7}) = 5.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{M}\), or equivalently from \(\text{pOH} = 14 - \text{pH}\); both ignore that \(\text{p}K_w = 13\) at \(60\,^\circ\text{C}\), and the resulting \([\text{OH}^-] = 5.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{M}\) falsely appears smaller than \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\), yielding an acidic label. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must chain the temperature-specific \(K_w\) through a sequence of calculations and use the derived neutral point, rather than the standard \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\) reference, to interpret the result.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'b01d331b-8243-4743-a606-4ab6839b6318';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b01d331b-8243-4743-a606-4ab6839b6318';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b01d331b-8243-4743-a606-4ab6839b6318', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reports the sample's pH (6.70) in the pOH slot, interchanging pH and pOH while keeping the (correct) basic classification$a$),
  ('b01d331b-8243-4743-a606-4ab6839b6318', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Computes pOH correctly but classifies acid-base character against the 25 C neutral value [H3O+]=1.0e-7 M instead of the temperature-shifted neutral point sqrt(Kw)=3.2e-7 M (dropped controlling constraint)$a$),
  ('b01d331b-8243-4743-a606-4ab6839b6318', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Uses the 25 C Kw=1.0e-14 (equivalently pOH=14-pH) instead of the stated 60 C Kw=1.0e-13, giving pOH 7.30 and an acidic label because the resulting [OH-] wrongly appears smaller than [H3O+]$a$);

-- 5. [Autoionization, Kw & the pH/pOH Scales · Skill 2 · ans D] id e177d5fc-db9e-4206-b93c-9d4678d891a2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist prepares a solution by adding hydrochloric acid to pure water at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\), where the autoionization of water gives \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\), to make the formal HCl concentration exactly \(1.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{M}\). Because HCl is a strong acid, it dissociates completely, so \([\text{Cl}^-] = 1.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{M}\). Taking proper account of the contribution of water's autoionization to the hydronium ion concentration, the pH of this solution is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(6.00\\), acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(8.00\\), slightly basic"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(7.00\\), neutral"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(6.98\\), slightly acidic"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because at such a low acid concentration the hydronium ions supplied by water's own autoionization are comparable to those from the acid, so \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) cannot be read directly off the formal acid concentration. The correct approach uses charge balance, which requires the total positive charge to equal the total negative charge: \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = [\text{Cl}^-] + [\text{OH}^-]\). Substituting \([\text{Cl}^-] = 1.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{M}\) and \([\text{OH}^-] = K_w/[\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) gives \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] = 1.0\times10^{-8} + \dfrac{1.0\times10^{-14}}{[\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]}\). Letting \(x = [\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) and multiplying through by \(x\) yields the quadratic \(x^2 - (1.0\times10^{-8})x - 1.0\times10^{-14} = 0\). Solving with the quadratic formula, \(x = \dfrac{1.0\times10^{-8} + \sqrt{(1.0\times10^{-8})^2 + 4(1.0\times10^{-14})}}{2} \approx 1.05\times10^{-7}\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pH} = -\log(1.05\times10^{-7}) \approx 6.98\). The result must lie just below \(7\): adding any strong acid to water can only raise \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\) above its neutral value, so the solution is necessarily acidic, though only slightly because the acid is so dilute. (Choice A) \(6.00\) arises from a mis-setup that computes \(K_w/[\text{acid}] = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(1.0\times10^{-8}) = 1.0\times10^{-6}\) and reports \(-\log(1.0\times10^{-6})\) as the pH; this misapplies the ion-product relation to the formal acid concentration rather than solving the charge-balance condition for \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+]\). (Choice B) \(8.00\) comes from naively evaluating \(-\log(1.0\times10^{-8})\), treating the acid as the sole hydronium source and ignoring water's autoionization entirely; this is not merely imprecise but qualitatively impossible, since it claims that adding a strong acid to neutral water produces a basic solution. (Choice C) \(7.00\) assumes the \(1.0\times10^{-8}\ \text{M}\) of acid is negligible and leaves the water effectively neutral; while the acid is indeed a small perturbation, it is not zero, and neglecting it discards exactly the acidic shift the problem is testing. This question tests Skill 2: Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'e177d5fc-db9e-4206-b93c-9d4678d891a2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e177d5fc-db9e-4206-b93c-9d4678d891a2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e177d5fc-db9e-4206-b93c-9d4678d891a2', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Mis-applies the ion product as Kw/[acid] = 1.0e-6 and reports -log of that value as the pH, instead of solving the charge-balance quadratic for [H3O+]$a$),
  ('e177d5fc-db9e-4206-b93c-9d4678d891a2', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Applies pH = -log(C_acid) directly, ignoring water autoionization at very low acid concentration (yields the impossible result that a strong acid gives a basic pH of 8)$a$),
  ('e177d5fc-db9e-4206-b93c-9d4678d891a2', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Treats the trace 1.0e-8 M acid as negligible, concluding the solution stays neutral at pH 7$a$);

-- 6. [Bicarbonate Buffer & Blood-pH Physiology · Skill 4 · ans A] id a5962a39-c2e9-4611-b1d4-4963771133eb
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A patient in metabolic acidosis has a plasma bicarbonate of \([\text{HCO}_3^-] = 12\ \text{mmol/L}\); to compensate, the patient hyperventilates until arterial \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) falls to \(26\ \text{mmHg}\). For the bicarbonate buffer, \(\text{pH} = 6.1 + \log\!\left(\dfrac{[\text{HCO}_3^-]}{0.03 \times P_{\text{CO}_2}}\right)\), with \([\text{HCO}_3^-]\) in mmol/L and \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) in mmHg. Given these values, the patient's arterial pH is most nearly$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(7.29\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(7.10\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(7.59\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(5.76\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Structure and integrative functions of the main organ systems'. The bicarbonate buffer is governed by the Henderson–Hasselbalch relationship written for the open carbonic-acid system, in which the denominator is not a directly measured acid concentration but the dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\), obtained by multiplying the partial pressure \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) by the solubility coefficient \(0.03\ \text{mmol·L}^{-1}\text{·mmHg}^{-1}\). The answer to this question is A because the calculation must chain three steps in order. First, convert the respiratory variable to a concentration: dissolved \(\text{CO}_2 = 0.03 \times 26 = 0.78\ \text{mmol/L}\). Second, form the conjugate-base-to-acid ratio using the metabolically reduced bicarbonate: \(\dfrac{[\text{HCO}_3^-]}{0.03 \times P_{\text{CO}_2}} = \dfrac{12}{0.78} \approx 15.4\). Third, take the logarithm and add it to \(\text{pK}_a\): \(\text{pH} = 6.1 + \log(15.4) = 6.1 + 1.19 \approx 7.29\). The result is acidemic (below \(7.4\)) because respiratory compensation only partially offsets the loss of bicarbonate; the lungs cannot fully normalize a metabolic disturbance. (Choice B) A pH of \(7.10\) results from ignoring the respiratory compensation entirely and inserting a normal \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) of \(40\ \text{mmHg}\): \(6.1 + \log\!\left(\frac{12}{0.03\times40}\right) = 6.1 + \log(10) = 7.10\), which fails to credit the hyperventilation that lowered \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) to \(26\ \text{mmHg}\). (Choice C) A pH of \(7.59\) results from using a normal bicarbonate of \(24\ \text{mmol/L}\) rather than the acid-depleted value of \(12\): \(6.1 + \log\!\left(\frac{24}{0.78}\right) \approx 7.59\), which overlooks the metabolic consumption of bicarbonate that defines the acidosis. (Choice D) A pH of \(5.76\) results from omitting the \(0.03\) solubility coefficient and dividing the bicarbonate by the raw \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\): \(6.1 + \log\!\left(\frac{12}{26}\right) \approx 5.76\); this collapses the open-system relationship because \(P_{\text{CO}_2}\) in mmHg must be converted to dissolved \(\text{CO}_2\) in mmol/L before the ratio is physically meaningful. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must convert a respiratory pressure into a dissolved-gas concentration, combine it with the metabolically altered bicarbonate, and propagate the intermediate ratio through a logarithm to reach the final pH.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'a5962a39-c2e9-4611-b1d4-4963771133eb';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a5962a39-c2e9-4611-b1d4-4963771133eb';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a5962a39-c2e9-4611-b1d4-4963771133eb', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Ignores the respiratory compensation and substitutes a normal P_CO2 of 40 mmHg instead of the stated compensated 26 mmHg, yielding pH 7.10$a$),
  ('a5962a39-c2e9-4611-b1d4-4963771133eb', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Uses a normal bicarbonate of 24 mmol/L instead of the acid-depleted 12 mmol/L given in the stem, dropping the defining metabolic constraint and yielding pH 7.59$a$),
  ('a5962a39-c2e9-4611-b1d4-4963771133eb', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Omits the 0.03 solubility coefficient and divides HCO3 by raw P_CO2 in mmHg, a unit/scale slip that yields pH 5.76$a$);

-- 7. [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · Skill 2 · ans D] id 159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A 1.00 L buffer holds 0.20 mol benzoic acid (\(\text{HA}\), \(\text{p}K_a = 4.20\)) and 0.20 mol benzoate (\(\text{A}^-\)). A chemist adds 0.30 mol solid \(\text{NaOH}\), which reacts to completion (treat the volume change as negligible). What is the \(\text{pH}\) of the resulting solution most likely closest to?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 4.90\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.90\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 13.48\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 13.00\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because the added strong base exceeds the buffer's acid reserve, so the buffer is destroyed and the leftover strong base, not the Henderson–Hasselbalch ratio, controls the \(\text{pH}\). The controlling step is the capacity check: \(\text{NaOH}\) reacts as \(\text{HA} + \text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{A}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O}\), but only 0.20 mol \(\text{HA}\) is present while 0.30 mol \(\text{OH}^-\) is added. The \(\text{OH}^-\) first consumes all 0.20 mol \(\text{HA}\) (producing 0.20 mol more \(\text{A}^-\), for 0.40 mol total), and \(0.30 - 0.20 = 0.10\ \text{mol}\) of strong \(\text{OH}^-\) remains in excess. Because Henderson–Hasselbalch requires both conjugate members to be present and the buffer no longer contains any \(\text{HA}\), the equation does not apply; the \(\text{pH}\) is fixed by the leftover strong base. In 1.00 L, \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.10\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pOH} = -\log(0.10) = 1.00\) and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 1.00 = 13.00\). The 0.40 mol \(\text{A}^-\) is the conjugate base of a weak acid and contributes negligibly to \([\text{OH}^-]\) against the 0.10 M strong-base background. (Choice A) \(4.90\) comes from applying Henderson–Hasselbalch blindly without the capacity check, taking \([\text{A}^-] = 0.20 + 0.30 = 0.50\) and \(\lvert 0.20 - 0.30\rvert = 0.10\) for \(\text{HA}\) to get \(4.20 + \log(5) = 4.90\); this treats a negative residual acid amount as if it were a real positive concentration. (Choice B) \(8.90\) arises from correctly noting that all \(\text{HA}\) is consumed but then discarding the 0.10 mol of leftover strong \(\text{OH}^-\), instead computing the \(\text{pH}\) of the 0.40 M \(\text{A}^-\) acting alone as a weak base via \(K_b = K_w/K_a\); this ignores that excess strong base swamps the weak-base hydrolysis. (Choice C) \(13.48\) results from recognizing that excess strong base sets the \(\text{pH}\) but using the full 0.30 mol of added \(\text{OH}^-\) as if none had been spent neutralizing the acid, giving \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.30\ \text{M}\) and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 0.52 = 13.48\); this omits the 0.20 mol consumed by \(\text{HA}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a stoichiometric capacity check with strong-base \(\text{pH}\) calculation and recognize when the Henderson–Hasselbalch principle no longer governs.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Applies Henderson–Hasselbalch without checking buffer capacity, plugging a negative residual acid amount as a positive concentration$a$),
  ('159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly consumes all weak acid but drops the leftover strong base and computes pH from the conjugate base hydrolysis alone$a$),
  ('159cc4ad-a0bf-4b76-aa1e-038d918a3a69', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Identifies excess strong base as governing but uses all added OH- instead of subtracting the moles spent neutralizing the acid$a$);

-- 8. [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · Skill 2 · ans C] id 32ecb909-d3b7-41bb-a9bd-bbba10c0f638
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(1.00\ \text{L}\) lactic-acid/lactate buffer (lactic acid \(\text{p}K_a = 3.86\)) is prepared with a total buffer concentration of \(0.50\ \text{M}\) (lactic acid plus lactate combined) and an initial \(\text{pH}\) of \(4.46\). A chemist then adds \(0.10\ \text{mol}\) of solid \(\text{HCl}\), which reacts completely with the buffer (treat the volume change as negligible). What is the \(\text{pH}\) of the buffer after the addition?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 3.68\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 4.46\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 4.04\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 3.49\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the problem must be solved in four chained steps. First, the initial \(\text{pH}\) fixes the conjugate ratio through the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation, \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log\frac{[\text{A}^-]}{[\text{HA}]}\): since \(4.46 - 3.86 = 0.60\) and \(\log^{-1}(0.60) = 4\), the initial ratio is \(\frac{[\text{lactate}]}{[\text{lactic acid}]} = 4\). Second, this ratio is combined with the total-concentration constraint: if \([\text{lactic acid}] = x\) then \([\text{lactate}] = 4x\), so \(x + 4x = 0.50\ \text{M}\) gives \(x = 0.10\ \text{M}\). In \(1.00\ \text{L}\) this is \(0.10\ \text{mol}\) lactic acid and \(0.40\ \text{mol}\) lactate. Third, the added strong acid is neutralized by the conjugate base: \(\text{HCl} + \text{lactate} \rightarrow \text{lactic acid} + \text{Cl}^-\), so \(0.10\ \text{mol}\ \text{HCl}\) consumes \(0.10\ \text{mol}\) lactate and creates \(0.10\ \text{mol}\) lactic acid, leaving \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) lactate and \(0.20\ \text{mol}\) lactic acid. Fourth, the new amounts return to Henderson–Hasselbalch: \(\text{pH} = 3.86 + \log\frac{0.30}{0.20} = 3.86 + \log(1.5) = 3.86 + 0.18 = 4.04\). (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 3.68\) arises from inverting the final Henderson–Hasselbalch ratio, computing \(3.86 + \log\frac{0.20}{0.30} = 3.86 - 0.18\); this places lactic acid over lactate after the reaction and drives the \(\text{pH}\) too far below the \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 4.46\) is the unchanged initial \(\text{pH}\); it results from stopping after the back-calculation of the conjugate amounts and never carrying out the strong-acid neutralization, ignoring that adding \(\text{HCl}\) converts lactate to lactic acid and lowers the ratio. (Choice D) \(\text{pH} = 3.49\) skips the ratio-and-total back-calculation and instead assumes the buffer began at a \(1:1\) composition (\(0.25\ \text{mol}\) of each), so the same \(0.10\ \text{mol}\ \text{HCl}\) leaves \(0.15\ \text{mol}\) lactate and \(0.35\ \text{mol}\) lactic acid, giving \(3.86 + \log\frac{0.15}{0.35} = 3.49\); this ignores that the stated initial \(\text{pH}\) of \(4.46\) requires a \(4:1\) starting ratio, not \(1:1\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation, a total-concentration constraint, and strong-acid neutralization stoichiometry, feeding each intermediate result into the next step before computing the final \(\text{pH}\).$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '32ecb909-d3b7-41bb-a9bd-bbba10c0f638';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '32ecb909-d3b7-41bb-a9bd-bbba10c0f638';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('32ecb909-d3b7-41bb-a9bd-bbba10c0f638', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Inverts the Henderson-Hasselbalch ratio after the reaction, placing the acid over the conjugate base so the pH shifts below the pKa instead of above the post-reaction value$a$),
  ('32ecb909-d3b7-41bb-a9bd-bbba10c0f638', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Stops after back-solving the initial conjugate amounts and reports the unchanged initial pH, omitting the strong-acid neutralization step entirely$a$),
  ('32ecb909-d3b7-41bb-a9bd-bbba10c0f638', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Skips the back-solve from the given pH plus total concentration and wrongly assumes a 1:1 starting buffer, then applies the neutralization to equal initial amounts$a$);

-- 9. [Buffers & Henderson-Hasselbalch · Skill 2 · ans A] id d2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(1.00\ \text{L}\) ammonia buffer made from \(\text{NH}_3\) and \(\text{NH}_4^+\) (\(\text{p}K_a(\text{NH}_4^+) = 9.25\)) is found to have an initial \(\text{pH}\) of \(9.55\), and the total amount of the two ammonia species together is \(0.60\ \text{mol}\). After \(0.25\ \text{mol}\) \(\text{HCl}\) is added (no volume change), what is the \(\text{pH}\) of the buffer most nearly?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.77\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 9.73\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 9.55\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.21\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because the problem must be solved in three chained steps. Step 1 (recover the starting composition): the given \(\text{pH}\) is not equal to the \(\text{p}K_a\), so the buffer is not equimolar, and the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation must be inverted to find the ratio: \(\log\!\left(\frac{[\text{NH}_3]}{[\text{NH}_4^+]}\right) = \text{pH} - \text{p}K_a = 9.55 - 9.25 = 0.30\), so \(\frac{[\text{NH}_3]}{[\text{NH}_4^+]} = 10^{0.30} = 2.0\). Combining the ratio \(2{:}1\) with the constraint that the two species total \(0.60\ \text{mol}\) gives \([\text{NH}_3] = 0.40\ \text{mol}\) and \([\text{NH}_4^+] = 0.20\ \text{mol}\). Step 2 (strong-acid stoichiometry): the \(0.25\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}^+\) reacts essentially completely with the base, \(\text{NH}_3 + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{NH}_4^+\); because \(0.25\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}^+ < 0.40\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_3\), the base is the limiting reactant but is not fully consumed, leaving \([\text{NH}_3] = 0.40 - 0.25 = 0.15\ \text{mol}\) and \([\text{NH}_4^+] = 0.20 + 0.25 = 0.45\ \text{mol}\). Step 3 (reapply Henderson–Hasselbalch): \(\text{pH} = 9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.15}{0.45}\right) = 9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{1}{3}\right) = 9.25 + (-0.48) = 8.77\). (Choice B) \(9.73\) comes from carrying out the correct stoichiometry but inverting the logarithm term, \(9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.45}{0.15}\right) = 9.25 + \log(3)\); placing the conjugate acid over the base reverses the direction of the shift, so adding acid would appear to raise the \(\text{pH}\), which is impossible. (Choice C) \(9.55\) is simply the initial \(\text{pH}\) reported unchanged; it follows from completing Step 1 but omitting the neutralization, ignoring that the added \(\text{H}^+\) shifts the ratio toward the conjugate acid and must lower the \(\text{pH}\). (Choice D) \(8.21\) results from skipping Step 1 and assuming the buffer started equimolar—splitting the \(0.60\ \text{mol}\) total evenly into \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of each species—then neutralizing to \(0.05\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_3\) and \(0.55\ \text{mol}\ \text{NH}_4^+\) for \(9.25 + \log\!\left(\frac{0.05}{0.55}\right)\); this discards the controlling constraint that the given \(\text{pH}\) of \(9.55 eq\text{p}K_a\) fixes a \(2{:}1\) starting ratio, not a \(1{:}1\) one. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must invert the Henderson–Hasselbalch relation to recover an unknown buffer composition, feed that result through a limiting-reactant neutralization, and reapply the relation to obtain the final \(\text{pH}\).$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'd2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Performs correct stoichiometry but inverts the Henderson-Hasselbalch log term (conjugate acid over base), making added acid appear to raise the pH$a$),
  ('d2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Completes the back-calculation of initial composition but omits the strong-acid neutralization step entirely, reporting the unchanged initial pH$a$),
  ('d2c0b2c1-669f-411c-a58c-376cec2fe17f', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Drops the controlling pH constraint and assumes an equimolar 1:1 starting buffer, splitting the total evenly instead of deriving the 2:1 ratio from the given pH$a$);

-- 10. [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · Skill 4 · ans A] id 3febbef5-3560-4a5a-8a54-204485c94217
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist compares the conjugate bases of two weak acids at 25 °C: nitrous acid (\(\text{HNO}_2\), \(K_a = 4.5 \times 10^{-4}\)) and hypochlorous acid (\(\text{HClO}\), \(\text{p}K_a = 7.5\)). Of the two conjugate bases, which is the stronger base, and what is its \(K_b\)? (\(K_w = 1.0 \times 10^{-14}\))$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{ClO}^-\\), with \\(K_b = 3.2 \\times 10^{-7}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{NO}_2^-\\), with \\(K_b = 2.2 \\times 10^{-11}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{ClO}^-\\), with \\(K_b = 3.2 \\times 10^{-8}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{ClO}^-\\), with \\(K_b = 1.3 \\times 10^{-15}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because the relative strength of two conjugate bases is governed by the inverse-strength relationship within each conjugate pair, \(K_a \times K_b = K_w\), so the conjugate base of the weaker parent acid (the smaller \(K_a\)) is the stronger base. To compare the two parent acids on a common footing, the \(\text{p}K_a = 7.5\) of \(\text{HClO}\) must first be converted to a \(K_a\): \(K_a = 10^{-7.5} = 3.2 \times 10^{-8}\). Comparing \(K_a(\text{HClO}) = 3.2 \times 10^{-8}\) with \(K_a(\text{HNO}_2) = 4.5 \times 10^{-4}\) shows \(\text{HClO}\) is the weaker acid by roughly four orders of magnitude, so its conjugate base \(\text{ClO}^-\) is the stronger base. Its \(K_b\) follows from \(K_b = K_w/K_a = (1.0 \times 10^{-14})/(3.2 \times 10^{-8}) = 3.2 \times 10^{-7}\). (Choice B) \(\text{NO}_2^-\) with \(K_b = 2.2 \times 10^{-11}\) reverses the inverse-strength relationship: the value is the correct \(K_b\) of nitrite (\(K_w/4.5 \times 10^{-4}\)), but \(\text{NO}_2^-\) is the conjugate base of the stronger parent acid and is therefore the weaker, not the stronger, base. (Choice C) \(\text{ClO}^-\) with \(K_b = 3.2 \times 10^{-8}\) correctly identifies the stronger base but reports the \(K_a\) of the parent acid \(\text{HClO}\) (the value obtained from \(10^{-7.5}\)) rather than carrying out the final division by \(K_w\); the quantity asked for is \(K_b\) of the conjugate base, which requires \(K_b = K_w/K_a\). (Choice D) \(\text{ClO}^-\) with \(K_b = 1.3 \times 10^{-15}\) arises from skipping the \(\text{p}K_a\)-to-\(K_a\) conversion and instead dividing \(K_w\) by the numerical value 7.5, treating \(\text{p}K_a\) as if it were \(K_a\); because \(\text{p}K_a = -\log K_a\), the constant must be exponentiated first, giving \(K_a = 3.2 \times 10^{-8}\) and a \(K_b\) eight orders of magnitude larger than this erroneous result. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must convert a logarithmic constant to its linear form, chain it through the water-ion-product relationship, and compare across two species to identify both the correct conjugate base and its quantitative \(K_b\).$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '3febbef5-3560-4a5a-8a54-204485c94217';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3febbef5-3560-4a5a-8a54-204485c94217';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3febbef5-3560-4a5a-8a54-204485c94217', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Assumes the stronger parent acid yields the stronger conjugate base, picking nitrite and reporting its (correctly computed) Kb$a$),
  ('3febbef5-3560-4a5a-8a54-204485c94217', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Stops after converting pKa to the parent acid's Ka and reports that Ka as the conjugate base's Kb, omitting the final Kw/Ka division$a$),
  ('3febbef5-3560-4a5a-8a54-204485c94217', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Treats pKa = 7.5 as if it were the linear Ka value, dividing Kw by 7.5 instead of exponentiating the logarithmic constant first$a$);

-- 11. [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · Skill 2 · ans B] id 83df2a3e-eac5-4ff2-bfa1-a3e1e1a2aeb5
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Four metal cations are each dissolved separately in water at the same concentration. None of the bare cations carries an ionizable proton of its own, yet every solution tests acidic to a different degree: the positive charge of the central cation pulls electron density out of the \(\text{O--H}\) bonds of the water molecules bound to it, and the more a coordinated \(\text{O--H}\) bond is polarized, the more readily that water releases an \(\text{H}^+\) to the bulk solvent. How strongly a cation polarizes its bound water tracks its charge density, the ratio of ionic charge to ionic radius. Given the ionic radii \(\text{Be}^{2+}\) (\(27\ \text{pm}\)), \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) (\(53\ \text{pm}\)), \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) (\(100\ \text{pm}\)), and \(\text{Na}^+\) (\(102\ \text{pm}\)), the hydrated form of which cation is most likely the strongest Bronsted-Lowry acid, with the lowest \(\text{p}K_a\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The hydrated \\(\\text{Al}^{3+}\\) ion, because it carries the largest charge of the four cations, and the cation with the greatest charge necessarily polarizes its bound \\(\\text{O--H}\\) bonds the most."},{"label":"B","text":"The hydrated \\(\\text{Be}^{2+}\\) ion, because its charge packed onto by far the smallest radius gives the highest charge density, polarizing its bound \\(\\text{O--H}\\) bonds the most."},{"label":"C","text":"The hydrated \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) ion, because it shares the same \\(+2\\) charge as the most polarizing cation, so it must polarize its bound \\(\\text{O--H}\\) bonds to the same degree."},{"label":"D","text":"The hydrated \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) ion, because its large radius lets its charge spread over and loosen more of the surrounding \\(\\text{O--H}\\) bonds than a tightly held charge could."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because the acidity of a hydrated cation is set by how strongly the central ion pulls electron density out of the \(\text{O--H}\) bonds of its bound water, and that polarizing power is measured by charge density, the ratio of charge to radius (\(z/r\)), not by charge alone. Computing \(z/r\) in charge per pm: \(\text{Na}^+\) gives \(1/102 \approx 0.0098\), \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) gives \(2/100 = 0.020\), \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) gives \(3/53 \approx 0.057\), and \(\text{Be}^{2+}\) gives \(2/27 \approx 0.074\). Although \(\text{Be}^{2+}\) carries only a \(+2\) charge, its radius is so small that its charge density is the highest of the four and exceeds even that of the \(+3\) \(\text{Al}^{3+}\); it therefore polarizes its bound \(\text{O--H}\) bonds the most, gives up \(\text{H}^+\) most readily, and its hydrated form has the lowest \(\text{p}K_a\). (Choice A) This stops at the size of the charge: \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) does carry the largest charge, but charge density depends on radius as well, and \(\text{Al}^{3+}\) (\(z/r \approx 0.057\)) sits below \(\text{Be}^{2+}\) (\(z/r \approx 0.074\)), so it is the second strongest acid rather than the strongest. (Choice C) This matches on charge while ignoring radius: \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) shares the \(+2\) charge of \(\text{Be}^{2+}\), but its much larger \(100\ \text{pm}\) radius gives it a charge density of only about \(0.020\), far below the \(0.074\) of \(\text{Be}^{2+}\), so equal charge does not produce equal polarizing power. (Choice D) This reverses the relationship between charge density and acidity: the \(+1\) charge of \(\text{Na}^+\) spread over the largest radius gives the lowest charge density of the four (about \(0.0098\)), so its hydrated form is the weakest acid, essentially neutral, not the strongest. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the idea that a cation's charge polarizes its bound \(\text{O--H}\) bonds with the quantitative charge-to-size ratio, then resolve a deliberate cross-pull in which a lower-charged but very small cation outcompetes a higher-charged one.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '83df2a3e-eac5-4ff2-bfa1-a3e1e1a2aeb5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '83df2a3e-eac5-4ff2-bfa1-a3e1e1a2aeb5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('83df2a3e-eac5-4ff2-bfa1-a3e1e1a2aeb5', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Ranks by charge magnitude alone, picking the only +3 cation and failing to account for radius, so it misses that the much smaller +2 Be2+ has a higher charge density$a$),
  ('83df2a3e-eac5-4ff2-bfa1-a3e1e1a2aeb5', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Matches on charge among same-charge ions while ignoring radius, assuming equal charge gives equal charge density even though Ca2+ has nearly four times the radius of Be2+$a$),
  ('83df2a3e-eac5-4ff2-bfa1-a3e1e1a2aeb5', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Treats large radius and diffuse charge as increasing acidity, reversing the charge-density relationship; the lowest z/r ion is the least acidic, not the most$a$);

-- 12. [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs · Skill 2 · ans D] id f0c3fb12-028c-4675-91ed-a1d1a5b392fa
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Sodium bisulfite (\(\text{NaHSO}_3\)) is dissolved in water at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\). The amphiprotic \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) ion can act as a Bronsted-Lowry acid (\(\text{HSO}_3^- \rightleftharpoons \text{SO}_3^{2-} + \text{H}^+\)) or as a Bronsted-Lowry base (\(\text{HSO}_3^- + \text{H}^+ \rightleftharpoons \text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\)). For its parent diprotic acid, \(K_{a1}(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3) = 1.4\times10^{-2}\) and \(K_{a2}(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3) = 6.3\times10^{-8}\), with \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\). Which option correctly identifies which role of \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) predominates and is most likely to set the solution's pH?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The base role predominates (\\(\\text{pH} > 7\\)), because the relevant base constant \\(K_b = K_w/K_{a2} = 1.6\\times10^{-7}\\) exceeds the acid-role constant \\(K_{a2} = 6.3\\times10^{-8}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"The base role predominates (\\(\\text{pH} > 7\\)), because \\(\\text{HSO}_3^-\\) is the conjugate base of \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_3\\) and conjugate bases of weak acids always hydrolyze to give a basic solution."},{"label":"C","text":"Neither role predominates (\\(\\text{pH} = 7\\)), because \\(\\text{HSO}_3^-\\) donates and accepts protons with the same constant, \\(K_{a1} = 1.4\\times10^{-2}\\), so the two effects cancel."},{"label":"D","text":"The acid role predominates (\\(\\text{pH} < 7\\)), because the acid-role constant \\(K_{a2} = 6.3\\times10^{-8}\\) exceeds the relevant base constant \\(K_b = K_w/K_{a1} = 7.1\\times10^{-13}\\)."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because deciding the net behavior of an amphiprotic ion requires comparing the equilibrium constant for each of its two roles, and each role must be matched to the correct step of the parent diprotic acid. In its acid role, \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) donates the SECOND proton, \(\text{HSO}_3^- \rightleftharpoons \text{SO}_3^{2-} + \text{H}^+\), so the governing constant is \(K_{a2} = 6.3\times10^{-8}\). In its base role, \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) accepts a proton to reform \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\); this is the reverse of the FIRST dissociation, so the governing constant is \(K_b = K_w/K_{a1} = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(1.4\times10^{-2}) = 7.1\times10^{-13}\). Because \(K_{a2} = 6.3\times10^{-8}\) is roughly five orders of magnitude larger than \(K_b = 7.1\times10^{-13}\), the acid role wins: \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) loses protons to water more readily than it abstracts them, so \([\text{H}_3\text{O}^+] > [\text{OH}^-]\) and the solution is acidic (\(\text{pH} < 7\)). (Choice A) makes the central pairing error: it computes the base-role constant from \(K_{a2}\) instead of \(K_{a1}\). The base role reverses the first dissociation (forming \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\)), so \(K_b\) must come from \(K_{a1}\); using \(K_w/K_{a2} = 1.6\times10^{-7}\) describes hydrolysis of \(\text{SO}_3^{2-}\), not the proton-accepting behavior of \(\text{HSO}_3^-\), and inverts the comparison. (Choice B) applies a memorized rule outside its valid domain: although \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) is a conjugate base of \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\), it is also a weak acid in its own right, and for an amphiprotic species the dominant tendency is set by the larger of the two constants, which here is \(K_{a2}\); the blanket claim that such ions always give basic solutions ignores the required quantitative comparison. (Choice C) treats one constant as governing both directions, but proton donation and proton acceptance by \(\text{HSO}_3^-\) involve two different equilibria (\(K_{a2}\) for donation and \(K_w/K_{a1}\) for acceptance); they are not equal and do not cancel, and \(K_{a1}\) describes the loss of the first proton from \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_3\), a species not even present as the dominant solute here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the conjugate acid-base relationship with the \(K_a K_b = K_w\) identity, assign each role of the amphiprotic ion to the correct dissociation step, compute the two competing constants, and compare them to determine the controlling equilibrium.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'f0c3fb12-028c-4675-91ed-a1d1a5b392fa';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f0c3fb12-028c-4675-91ed-a1d1a5b392fa';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f0c3fb12-028c-4675-91ed-a1d1a5b392fa', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Pairs the base-role Kb with Ka2 (the wrong dissociation step) instead of Ka1, computing Kw/Ka2 = 1.6e-7 and reversing the dominance comparison to conclude the solution is basic.$a$),
  ('f0c3fb12-028c-4675-91ed-a1d1a5b392fa', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Applies the overgeneralized rule that conjugate bases of weak acids always make basic solutions, ignoring that an amphiprotic ion's net behavior requires comparing its acid-role and base-role constants.$a$),
  ('f0c3fb12-028c-4675-91ed-a1d1a5b392fa', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Assumes proton donation and proton acceptance are governed by the same single constant (Ka1) so they cancel to neutral, failing to recognize the two roles are distinct equilibria with different K values.$a$);

-- 13. [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · Skill 2 · ans D] id 87067bc6-b083-4740-bc89-2607bc9c18e3
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Two salts are dissolved in separate beakers at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\): trimethylammonium chloride (\(\text{(CH}_3\text{)}_3\text{NHCl}\)) at \(0.050\ \text{M}\), whose parent base trimethylamine has \(K_b = 6.3\times10^{-5}\), and methylammonium chloride (\(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_3\text{Cl}\)) at \(0.50\ \text{M}\), whose parent base methylamine has \(K_b = 4.4\times10^{-4}\). Chloride is a spectator in both, and in each solution only the cation hydrolyzes (\(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\); treat \(x\) as small). Using \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{K_a C}\), what is the \(\text{pH}\) of whichever solution is the more acidic of the two?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.83\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.97\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.55\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.47\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is D because identifying the more acidic solution requires chaining the conjugate-acid relationship to the hydrolysis equilibrium and then to the concentration of each salt. First, the cation acidity is set by \(K_a = K_w/K_b\) of the parent base: for trimethylammonium, \(K_a = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(6.3\times10^{-5}) = 1.6\times10^{-10}\); for methylammonium, \(K_a = (1.0\times10^{-14})/(4.4\times10^{-4}) = 2.3\times10^{-11}\). Trimethylammonium is therefore the stronger conjugate acid by a factor of about 7. However, the acidity actually produced depends on \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{K_a C}\), so the controlling quantity is the product \(K_a C\): for the trimethylammonium solution \(K_a C = (1.6\times10^{-10})(0.050) = 7.9\times10^{-12}\), while for the methylammonium solution \(K_a C = (2.3\times10^{-11})(0.50) = 1.1\times10^{-11}\). The methylammonium product is larger because its tenfold-higher concentration more than offsets its sevenfold-smaller \(K_a\), so the methylammonium solution is the more acidic one. Its \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{1.1\times10^{-11}} = 3.4\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\), giving \(\text{pH} = -\log(3.4\times10^{-6}) = 5.47\). (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 1.83\) results from using the parent-base \(K_b = 4.4\times10^{-4}\) directly as the cation's \(K_a\) instead of converting through \(K_a = K_w/K_b\); this omits the \(K_w\) factor and overstates the cation's acidity by roughly nine orders of magnitude. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 5.97\) uses the correct methylammonium \(K_a = 2.3\times10^{-11}\) but pairs it with the wrong concentration (\(0.050\ \text{M}\) rather than \(0.50\ \text{M}\)), as from swapping the two salts' concentrations, yielding \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{(2.3\times10^{-11})(0.050)} = 1.1\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\). (Choice C) \(\text{pH} = 5.55\) is the \(\text{pH}\) of the trimethylammonium solution (\(\sqrt{(1.6\times10^{-10})(0.050)} = 2.8\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\)); it follows from ranking the two solutions by cation \(K_a\) alone and concluding the larger-\(K_a\) cation must give the lower \(\text{pH}\), which ignores that \([\text{H}^+]\) also scales with the square root of concentration. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must convert each parent \(K_b\) to a cation \(K_a\), feed both \(K_a\) values and the differing concentrations into \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{K_a C}\), recognize that concentration reverses the acidity ranking that \(K_a\) alone would predict, and only then compute the \(\text{pH}\) of the correct solution.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '87067bc6-b083-4740-bc89-2607bc9c18e3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '87067bc6-b083-4740-bc89-2607bc9c18e3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('87067bc6-b083-4740-bc89-2607bc9c18e3', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Used the parent base's Kb directly as the cation's Ka, omitting the Ka = Kw/Kb conversion$a$),
  ('87067bc6-b083-4740-bc89-2607bc9c18e3', 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Correct cation Ka for the right solution but paired with the swapped concentration (0.050 M instead of 0.50 M)$a$),
  ('87067bc6-b083-4740-bc89-2607bc9c18e3', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Ranked the solutions by cation Ka alone and dropped the concentration term in [H+] = sqrt(Ka*C), landing on the less-acidic (larger-Ka, lower-concentration) solution$a$);

-- 14. [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · Skill 4 · ans C] id e508389f-fcfb-4c42-ae25-61b226224b82
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Two salts of strong-base/weak-acid origin are dissolved in separate beakers at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\): sodium phenoxide (\(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{ONa}\), parent acid phenol \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{OH}\), \(K_a = 1.0\times10^{-10}\)) is prepared at \(0.025\ \text{M}\), while sodium benzoate (\(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{COONa}\), parent acid benzoic acid \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{COOH}\), \(K_a = 6.3\times10^{-5}\)) is prepared at the tenfold larger concentration of \(0.25\ \text{M}\). In each beaker only the anion hydrolyzes and \(\text{Na}^+\) is a spectator (\(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\)). Using the approximation \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{K_b\,C}\), the more basic solution will most likely have a \(\text{pH}\) closest to$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 11.70\\), the sodium phenoxide solution"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.80\\), the sodium benzoate solution"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 11.20\\), the sodium phenoxide solution"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.20\\), the sodium phenoxide solution"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because the basicity of a strong-base/weak-acid salt is set by the conjugate-base strength of its anion, expressed through \(K_b = K_w/K_a\), and the resulting \([\text{OH}^-]\) must then be computed at each salt's actual concentration before the two solutions can be compared. First, convert each parent \(K_a\) to the anion \(K_b\): for phenoxide \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{O}^-\), \(K_b = 1.0\times10^{-14}/1.0\times10^{-10} = 1.0\times10^{-4}\); for benzoate \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{COO}^-\), \(K_b = 1.0\times10^{-14}/6.3\times10^{-5} = 1.6\times10^{-10}\). The phenoxide anion is the far stronger base, by roughly six orders of magnitude, because phenol is the much weaker parent acid. Next apply \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{K_b\,C}\) at the stated concentrations. For \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{O}^-\) at \(0.025\ \text{M}\): \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{(1.0\times10^{-4})(0.025)} = 1.6\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\), giving \(\text{pOH} = 2.80\) and \(\text{pH} = 11.20\). For \(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{COO}^-\) at \(0.25\ \text{M}\): \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{(1.6\times10^{-10})(0.25)} = 6.3\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\), giving \(\text{pOH} = 5.20\) and \(\text{pH} = 8.80\). Even though the benzoate is tenfold more concentrated, the \(K_b\) gap dominates the square-root dependence, so the sodium phenoxide solution is the more basic at \(\text{pH} = 11.20\). (Choice A) This applies the correct anion (\(\text{C}_6\text{H}_5\text{O}^-\)) and correct \(K_b\) but inserts \(C = 0.25\ \text{M}\) instead of the phenoxide's actual \(0.025\ \text{M}\), inflating \([\text{OH}^-]\) by \(\sqrt{10}\) and overstating the \(\text{pH}\) by half a unit to \(11.70\). (Choice B) This is the correct \(\text{pH}\) of the benzoate solution but selects it as the more basic by reasoning that the higher concentration (\(0.25\ \text{M}\)) wins; concentration enters only under a square root, so a tenfold edge raises \([\text{OH}^-]\) by about \(3.2\times\), which cannot overcome the \(\sim10^{6}\) deficit in \(K_b\), leaving benzoate the less basic solution. (Choice D) This uses the correct anion and concentration but substitutes the parent acid's \(K_a = 1.0\times10^{-10}\) directly as the base constant rather than converting it through \(K_b = K_w/K_a\); using the much smaller \(K_a\) underestimates \([\text{OH}^-]\) and yields a \(\text{pH}\) of \(8.20\). This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must chain a conjugate-pair conversion, an equilibrium calculation at two distinct concentrations, and a \(\text{pOH}\)-to-\(\text{pH}\) step, then judge which computed value is larger.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'e508389f-fcfb-4c42-ae25-61b226224b82';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e508389f-fcfb-4c42-ae25-61b226224b82';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e508389f-fcfb-4c42-ae25-61b226224b82', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Uses the correct phenoxide Kb but substitutes the benzoate concentration (0.25 M) instead of phenoxide's 0.025 M, inflating [OH-] by sqrt(10)$a$),
  ('e508389f-fcfb-4c42-ae25-61b226224b82', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Selects the more concentrated salt as more basic, ignoring that concentration enters only under a square root and cannot overcome the Kb gap$a$),
  ('e508389f-fcfb-4c42-ae25-61b226224b82', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Substitutes the parent acid's Ka directly as the base constant instead of converting via Kb = Kw/Ka, underestimating [OH-]$a$);

-- 15. [Salt Hydrolysis & pH of Salt Solutions · Skill 2 · ans B] id f7cdd078-df0c-4ec7-8e32-cb22c56aa05f
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist dissolves \(3.40\ \text{g}\) of solid sodium formate (\(\text{HCOONa}\), molar mass \(68.0\ \text{g/mol}\)) in water and dilutes the solution to a total volume of \(250.0\ \text{mL}\) at \(25\,^\circ\text{C}\), where \(K_w = 1.0\times10^{-14}\) and the parent acid \(\text{HCOOH}\) has \(K_a = 1.8\times10^{-4}\). Treating the dissolved formate ion as the only species that reacts with water and assuming \(x\) is small relative to its concentration, what is the \(\text{pH}\) of the resulting solution most nearly?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 5.48\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.52\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 2.22\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 8.22\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because the calculation requires three chained steps: finding the formate concentration from the dissolved mass and the final diluted volume, converting \(K_a\) of the parent acid into \(K_b\) of the conjugate base, and then solving the base-hydrolysis equilibrium to a \(\text{pH}\). First, the moles of salt are \(n = \dfrac{3.40\ \text{g}}{68.0\ \text{g/mol}} = 0.0500\ \text{mol}\), and because the solution is diluted to \(250.0\ \text{mL} = 0.250\ \text{L}\), the formate concentration is \(C = \dfrac{0.0500\ \text{mol}}{0.250\ \text{L}} = 0.200\ \text{M}\). Sodium formate dissociates completely into \(\text{Na}^+\) (a spectator cation of the strong base \(\text{NaOH}\)) and \(\text{HCOO}^-\) (the conjugate base of the weak acid \(\text{HCOOH}\)), so \(\text{HCOO}^-\) hydrolyzes and the solution is basic. Its base ionization constant is \(K_b = \dfrac{K_w}{K_a} = \dfrac{1.0\times10^{-14}}{1.8\times10^{-4}} = 5.6\times10^{-11}\). For \(\text{HCOO}^- + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightleftharpoons \text{HCOOH} + \text{OH}^-\), the small-\(x\) approximation gives \([\text{OH}^-] = \sqrt{K_b\,C} = \sqrt{(5.6\times10^{-11})(0.200)} = \sqrt{1.1\times10^{-11}} = 3.3\times10^{-6}\ \text{M}\). Then \(\text{pOH} = -\log(3.3\times10^{-6}) = 5.48\), so \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 5.48 = 8.52\), a weakly basic value consistent with the conjugate base of a moderately weak acid. (Choice A) \(5.48\) is the \(\text{pOH}\) reported as if it were the \(\text{pH}\); this omits the final \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - \text{pOH}\) conversion and would mislabel a basic solution as acidic, which contradicts the basic character of a salt whose anion is a conjugate base. (Choice C) \(2.22\) arises from treating \(\text{HCOO}^-\) as a weak acid and computing \([\text{H}^+] = \sqrt{K_a\,C} = \sqrt{(1.8\times10^{-4})(0.200)} = 6.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}\); this uses the parent acid's \(K_a\) on the wrong species and reverses the acid-base role of the ion actually present in solution, which is the conjugate base \(\text{HCOO}^-\), not \(\text{HCOOH}\). (Choice D) \(8.22\) results from skipping the dilution step and using the \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of salt as though it were a \(0.0500\ \text{M}\) concentration in \(1\ \text{L}\); the correct concentration after dilution to \(250.0\ \text{mL}\) is four times larger at \(0.200\ \text{M}\), which raises \([\text{OH}^-]\) and the \(\text{pH}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a stoichiometric mole-to-concentration calculation with a \(K_a\)-to-\(K_b\) conversion and an equilibrium solve, identifying at each step which quantity and which species govern the result.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'f7cdd078-df0c-4ec7-8e32-cb22c56aa05f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f7cdd078-df0c-4ec7-8e32-cb22c56aa05f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f7cdd078-df0c-4ec7-8e32-cb22c56aa05f', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Computes pOH correctly but reports it as the pH, dropping the final pH = 14 - pOH conversion and mislabeling a basic salt solution as acidic.$a$),
  ('f7cdd078-df0c-4ec7-8e32-cb22c56aa05f', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Treats the conjugate base formate ion as a weak acid, applying the parent acid's Ka in [H+] = sqrt(Ka*C) instead of converting to Kb for the actual species in solution.$a$),
  ('f7cdd078-df0c-4ec7-8e32-cb22c56aa05f', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Skips the dilution-to-250 mL step, using the 0.0500 mol of salt as a 0.0500 M concentration instead of dividing by 0.250 L to get 0.200 M.$a$);

-- 16. [Strong vs Weak Acids/Bases; Ka and Kb · Skill 2 · ans B] id 1b14cfe5-3424-4e49-9557-e52bb407bc00
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist compiles two acidity trends for binary hydrides in water at 25 °C. Down group 17 the order is \(\text{HF} < \text{HCl} < \text{HBr} < \text{HI}\), even though electronegativity falls steadily from \(\text{F}\) to \(\text{I}\). Across period 2 the order is \(\text{CH}_4 < \text{NH}_3 < \text{H}_2\text{O} < \text{HF}\), tracking the rise in central-atom electronegativity from \(\text{C}\) to \(\text{F}\). Which single explanation best accounts for why acidity rises in BOTH directions even though the two series respond oppositely to electronegativity?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"In both series the \\(\\text{X–H}\\) bond weakens as acidity rises, so bond strength is the controlling factor throughout, and electronegativity merely tracks bond strength as a secondary correlate in each direction."},{"label":"B","text":"Down group 17 the \\(\\text{H–X}\\) bond lengthens and weakens, so bond strength governs and overrides the falling electronegativity; across period 2 the \\(\\text{X–H}\\) bonds are of comparable length, so electronegativity governs by polarizing the bond and stabilizing the conjugate base."},{"label":"C","text":"In both series the more electronegative central atom yields the stronger acid, so electronegativity is the controlling factor throughout; the group-17 order simply reflects that the heavier halogens are effectively more electronegative toward hydrogen."},{"label":"D","text":"Across period 2 the shortening \\(\\text{X–H}\\) bond is what raises acidity, while down group 17 the rising conjugate-base electronegativity is what raises acidity, so each series is governed by the factor that changes most steeply within it."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is B because the controlling factor for binary-hydride acidity is not fixed — it depends on the direction moved through the periodic table, and each given trend tells you which factor wins. Down group 17, electronegativity falls from \(\text{F}\) to \(\text{I}\), so it cannot be the driver of the observed rise in acidity; what changes dramatically is atomic size, which lengthens and weakens the \(\text{H–X}\) bond, so the proton is released more readily and acidity increases \(\text{HF} < \text{HCl} < \text{HBr} < \text{HI}\). Bond strength therefore governs down a group and overrides the opposing electronegativity trend. Across period 2, the central atoms \(\text{C}\), \(\text{N}\), \(\text{O}\), \(\text{F}\) are all in the same row and the \(\text{X–H}\) bonds are of comparable length, so bond-strength differences are minor; here the dominant variable is electronegativity, which both polarizes the \(\text{X–H}\) bond (making \(\text{H}\) more positive) and stabilizes the resulting conjugate base by concentrating negative charge on the more electronegative atom, so acidity increases \(\text{CH}_4 < \text{NH}_3 < \text{H}_2\text{O} < \text{HF}\). Recognizing that bond strength controls the down-group series while electronegativity controls the across-period series is exactly what reconciles the two opposite responses to electronegativity. (Choice A) Treating bond strength as the sole controlling factor in both series fails for period 2: the period-2 \(\text{X–H}\) bonds are of similar length, so their small bond-strength differences cannot produce the large acidity span, and bond strength does not even fall monotonically in the order required, so this choice cannot explain why \(\text{HF}\) is far more acidic than \(\text{CH}_4\). (Choice C) Treating electronegativity as the sole controlling factor in both series directly contradicts the group-17 data: electronegativity falls down the group while acidity rises, so the most electronegative \(\text{HF}\) would have to be the strongest hydrohalic acid, which is the opposite of the stated order, and the claim that heavier halogens are "effectively more electronegative" reverses the actual periodic trend. (Choice D) This choice assigns each factor to the wrong series: it credits a shortening bond for the period-2 trend (acidity actually rises there because of electronegativity, not because shorter bonds are easier to break — shorter bonds are stronger and harder to break) and credits rising conjugate-base electronegativity for the group-17 trend (electronegativity falls down the group, so it cannot be raising acidity there), so both assignments are inverted relative to the governing factor in each direction. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the down-group bond-strength trend with the across-period electronegativity trend and identify which factor controls in each direction to reconcile the two series.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '1b14cfe5-3424-4e49-9557-e52bb407bc00';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1b14cfe5-3424-4e49-9557-e52bb407bc00';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1b14cfe5-3424-4e49-9557-e52bb407bc00', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Over-generalizes the down-group rule (bond strength) to the across-period series, where comparable bond lengths make electronegativity the true driver$a$),
  ('1b14cfe5-3424-4e49-9557-e52bb407bc00', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Treats electronegativity as the universal determinant of binary-acid strength, directly contradicting the group-17 fact that acidity rises as electronegativity falls$a$),
  ('1b14cfe5-3424-4e49-9557-e52bb407bc00', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Swaps the two controlling factors between the series (bond length to the period, conjugate-base electronegativity to the group), inverting both assignments$a$);

-- 17. [Strong vs Weak Acids/Bases; Ka and Kb · Skill 2 · ans A] id 336cafe1-407b-4f94-bf22-752ee92591eb
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Four monoprotic carboxylic acids are compared in water at 25 °C: dichloroacetic acid \(\text{Cl}_2\text{CHCOOH}\), chloroacetic acid \(\text{ClCH}_2\text{COOH}\), fluoroacetic acid \(\text{FCH}_2\text{COOH}\), and 3-chloropropanoic acid \(\text{ClCH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{COOH}\). Each ionizes at the same carboxyl \(\text{O--H}\) bond, and no \(K_a\) values are provided. On the basis of conjugate-base stabilization by the inductive effect, the acids are most likely ranked from strongest to weakest as:$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2\\text{CHCOOH} > \\text{FCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{COOH}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2\\text{CHCOOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{FCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{COOH}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2\\text{CHCOOH} > \\text{FCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{COOH}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{FCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{Cl}_2\\text{CHCOOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{COOH} > \\text{ClCH}_2\\text{CH}_2\\text{COOH}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is A because acid strength is governed by the stability of the conjugate carboxylate: the more the developing negative charge is dispersed by electron-withdrawing inductive effects, the lower the anion's energy, the larger the \(K_a\), and the lower the \(\text{p}K_a\). Ranking these four acids requires chaining three separate inductive considerations. First, the NUMBER of withdrawing groups: \(\text{Cl}_2\text{CHCOOH}\) carries two \(\alpha\) chlorines, whose effects are additive, so it disperses charge most effectively and is the strongest acid. Second, among the two acids bearing a single halogen on the \(\alpha\) carbon, \(\text{FCH}_2\text{COOH}\) and \(\text{ClCH}_2\text{COOH}\), the discriminator is halogen ELECTRONEGATIVITY: fluorine (Pauling \(3.98\)) is more electronegative than chlorine (\(3.16\)), so it withdraws \(\sigma\)-density more strongly and stabilizes the carboxylate better, making \(\text{FCH}_2\text{COOH}\) the stronger of the two. Third, the DISTANCE of the substituent from the carboxyl: in \(\text{ClCH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{COOH}\) the chlorine sits on the \(\beta\) carbon, one bond farther from the \(\text{COO}^-\) center, and inductive effects attenuate sharply with each intervening \(\sigma\) bond, so its stabilization is weakest and it is the weakest acid of the set. Combining these gives \(\text{Cl}_2\text{CHCOOH} > \text{FCH}_2\text{COOH} > \text{ClCH}_2\text{COOH} > \text{ClCH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{COOH}\). (Choice B) This reverses the relative strengths of \(\text{FCH}_2\text{COOH}\) and \(\text{ClCH}_2\text{COOH}\) by assuming the larger, more polarizable chlorine withdraws more strongly than fluorine; in fact the controlling quantity for the \(\sigma\)-inductive effect is electronegativity, and fluorine's higher electronegativity makes \(\text{FCH}_2\text{COOH}\) the stronger acid. (Choice C) This drops the distance constraint, treating the \(\beta\)-chlorine of \(\text{ClCH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{COOH}\) as equivalent to a chlorine on the \(\alpha\) carbon; because inductive withdrawal falls off rapidly with each additional intervening bond, the \(\beta\)-substituted acid is in fact weaker than \(\alpha\)-chloroacetic acid, not stronger. (Choice D) This over-weights the single most electronegative atom, ranking the lone fluorine of \(\text{FCH}_2\text{COOH}\) above the two chlorines of \(\text{Cl}_2\text{CHCOOH}\); although fluorine is more electronegative per atom, two \(\alpha\) chlorines produce a larger combined withdrawal than one \(\alpha\) fluorine, so the additive count dominates and dichloroacetic acid is the strongest. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the number of withdrawing groups, the electronegativity of each halogen, and the bond-distance attenuation of the inductive effect to order conjugate-base stabilities without any tabulated \(K_a\) values.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '336cafe1-407b-4f94-bf22-752ee92591eb';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '336cafe1-407b-4f94-bf22-752ee92591eb';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('336cafe1-407b-4f94-bf22-752ee92591eb', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses halogen ranking by assuming larger/more-polarizable Cl withdraws more strongly than F, instead of using electronegativity (F > Cl)$a$),
  ('336cafe1-407b-4f94-bf22-752ee92591eb', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Drops the distance-attenuation constraint, treating a beta-carbon chlorine as equivalent to an alpha-carbon chlorine ('a Cl is a Cl')$a$),
  ('336cafe1-407b-4f94-bf22-752ee92591eb', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Over-weights the single most electronegative atom (one F) over the additive effect of two Cl, ignoring that withdrawing groups sum$a$);

-- 18. [Titration & Titration Curves · Skill 2 · ans D] id bd973aea-fb84-49a5-a847-f0b0a79bd408
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A 25.00 mL sample contains both \(0.100\,\text{M}\ \text{HCl}\) (a strong acid) and \(0.0500\,\text{M}\ \text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) (a weak acid, \(K_a = 1.8\times10^{-5}\)). The mixture is titrated with \(0.150\,\text{M}\ \text{NaOH}\) at 25 °C. After a total of 30.00 mL of titrant has been delivered, what is the \(\text{pH}\) of the resulting solution?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.87\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 12.40\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 12.56\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 12.13\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the strong base is in excess after every titratable proton has been neutralized, so the leftover \(\text{OH}^-\) sets the \(\text{pH}\) and the weak acid's \(K_a\) is irrelevant once it is fully converted to acetate. First count titratable protons: \(\text{HCl}\) supplies \(25.00\,\text{mL}\times0.100\,\text{M} = 2.500\,\text{mmol}\ \text{H}^+\), and \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) supplies \(25.00\,\text{mL}\times0.0500\,\text{M} = 1.250\,\text{mmol}\) of titratable acid; because a strong base drives even a weak acid to completion, the total base needed to neutralize all acid is \(2.500 + 1.250 = 3.750\,\text{mmol}\). The \(\text{NaOH}\) delivered is \(30.00\,\text{mL}\times0.150\,\text{M} = 4.500\,\text{mmol}\). Subtracting, the excess strong base is \(4.500 - 3.750 = 0.750\,\text{mmol}\ \text{OH}^-\). The total volume is \(25.00 + 30.00 = 55.00\,\text{mL}\), so \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.750\,\text{mmol}/55.00\,\text{mL} = 0.01364\,\text{M}\). Then \(\text{pOH} = -\log(0.01364) = 1.87\) and \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - 1.87 = 12.13\). (Choice A) \(1.87\) is the \(\text{pOH}\) of the solution reported as if it were the \(\text{pH}\); past complete neutralization the solution is strongly basic, so \(\text{pH} = 14.00 - \text{pOH} = 12.13\), not the \(\text{pOH}\) value itself. (Choice B) \(12.40\) results from dividing the correct excess \(0.750\,\text{mmol}\) by only the 30.00 mL of titrant added rather than the full 55.00 mL of solution, giving \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.0250\,\text{M}\) instead of \(0.01364\,\text{M}\). (Choice C) \(12.56\) comes from subtracting only the \(2.500\,\text{mmol}\) of \(\text{HCl}\) and ignoring that the strong base also fully consumes the \(1.250\,\text{mmol}\) of acetic acid, leaving an inflated excess of \(2.00\,\text{mmol}\ \text{OH}^-\) and \([\text{OH}^-] = 0.0364\,\text{M}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain mixed-acid stoichiometry, recognize that a strong titrant fully neutralizes a weak acid before any excess accumulates, and carry the excess-\(\text{OH}^-\) concentration through to a correct pH using the full solution volume.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'bd973aea-fb84-49a5-a847-f0b0a79bd408';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'bd973aea-fb84-49a5-a847-f0b0a79bd408';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('bd973aea-fb84-49a5-a847-f0b0a79bd408', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reports the calculated pOH directly as the pH instead of converting via pH = 14 - pOH for a basic solution$a$),
  ('bd973aea-fb84-49a5-a847-f0b0a79bd408', 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Divides the excess hydroxide moles by the titrant volume (30.00 mL) instead of the total solution volume (55.00 mL)$a$),
  ('bd973aea-fb84-49a5-a847-f0b0a79bd408', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Counts only the strong-acid protons and fails to subtract the base consumed by the weak acid, overstating the excess OH-$a$);

-- 19. [Titration & Titration Curves · Skill 2 · ans C] id f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(60.00\,\text{mL}\) sample of \(0.100\,\text{M}\) \(\text{HCl}\) is titrated with \(0.300\,\text{M}\) \(\text{NaOH}\). When the titration is exactly three-quarters of the way to the equivalence point, the \(\text{pH}\) of the solution is most nearly (treat both species as strong and neutralization as complete):$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.22\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.60\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.70\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH} = 1.85\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because a pre-equivalence point in a strong acid–strong base titration has its \(\text{pH}\) set by the leftover excess strong acid, and reaching it requires first finding the true equivalence volume from the mismatched concentrations and then diluting the excess over the total combined volume. First, the moles of acid: \(n_{\text{HCl}} = 0.06000\,\text{L} \times 0.100\,\text{M} = 6.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\). Because the titrant is three times as concentrated as the analyte, the equivalence volume is not half the acid volume; it is \(V_{\text{eq}} = \dfrac{6.00\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}}{0.300\,\text{M}} = 0.02000\,\text{L} = 20.0\,\text{mL}\). Three-quarters of the way to equivalence is \(V_{\text{NaOH}} = 0.75 \times 20.0\,\text{mL} = 15.0\,\text{mL}\), delivering \(n_{\text{NaOH}} = 0.300\,\text{M} \times 0.01500\,\text{L} = 4.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\). The added \(\text{OH}^-\) neutralizes an equal amount of \(\text{H}^+\), leaving excess \(\text{H}^+ = 6.00\times10^{-3} - 4.50\times10^{-3} = 1.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) (equivalently, one quarter of the original acid remains). The total volume is now \(60.0 + 15.0 = 75.0\,\text{mL} = 0.07500\,\text{L}\), so \([\text{H}^+] = \dfrac{1.50\times10^{-3}}{0.07500} = 0.0200\,\text{M}\) and \(\text{pH} = -\log(0.0200) = 1.70\). (Choice A) \(\text{pH} = 1.22\) reverses the fraction, taking "three-quarters of the way" to mean three-quarters of the acid still remains; this assigns excess \(\text{H}^+ = 0.75 \times 6.00\times10^{-3} = 4.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\), which over the correct \(0.07500\,\text{L}\) gives \([\text{H}^+] = 0.0600\,\text{M}\); in fact three-quarters to equivalence means three-quarters of the acid has been neutralized, so only one quarter remains. (Choice B) \(\text{pH} = 1.60\) uses the correct excess of \(1.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) but divides by the original acid volume \(0.06000\,\text{L}\) to get \(0.0250\,\text{M}\); this ignores that the \(15.0\,\text{mL}\) of added titrant dilutes the solution, so the total volume \(0.07500\,\text{L}\) must be used. (Choice D) \(\text{pH} = 1.85\) drops the controlling concentration mismatch and treats the titrant as if it were the same concentration as the acid, so "three-quarters of the way" is taken as \(0.75 \times 60.0\,\text{mL} = 45.0\,\text{mL}\) of titrant and the total volume as \(105.0\,\text{mL}\); dividing the excess \(1.50\times10^{-3}\,\text{mol}\) by \(0.10500\,\text{L}\) gives \(0.0143\,\text{M}\), but the \(0.300\,\text{M}\) titrant reaches equivalence at only \(20.0\,\text{mL}\), so the volume actually added is \(15.0\,\text{mL}\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the equivalence-volume relationship under unequal concentrations with the dilution of excess strong acid, chaining several intermediate quantities to reach the pH.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the fraction-to-equivalence, treating 'three-quarters of the way' as three-quarters of the acid remaining (excess = 0.0045 mol) rather than three-quarters neutralized, yielding [H+] = 0.0600 M.$a$),
  ('f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Dropped-dilution error: divides the correct excess H+ (0.0015 mol) by the original acid volume (60.0 mL) instead of the total combined volume (75.0 mL), the same dilution oversight the original easy item tested.$a$),
  ('f09ab786-81c5-4363-8aeb-dc3c08ba015c', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Ignores the controlling 3:1 concentration mismatch and computes the titrant volume as three-quarters of the acid volume (45.0 mL, total 105.0 mL) rather than recognizing the true equivalence volume is 20.0 mL.$a$);

-- 20. [Titration & Titration Curves · Skill 4 · ans A] id fd43f4f0-3620-4c15-84e8-0595648ab5df
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist titrates a \(25.00\ \text{mL}\) sample of \(0.100\ \text{M}\) malonic acid (\(\text{p}K_{a1}=2.8\), \(\text{p}K_{a2}=5.7\)) with \(0.100\ \text{M}\) \(\text{NaOH}\) at \(25\,^{\circ}\text{C}\). The chemist wants the indicator color change to fall as close as possible to the second equivalence point. At that point, what is the \(\text{pH}\) of the solution, and therefore over what range should the chosen indicator change color?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{pH}\\approx 9.11\\); the solution holds the malonate dianion, a weak base, diluted to \\(0.0333\\ \\text{M}\\), so an indicator changing near \\(\\text{pH}\\) 9 best brackets the endpoint."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{pH}\\approx 9.35\\); the malonate dianion acts as a weak base at its full \\(0.100\\ \\text{M}\\) formal concentration, so an indicator changing near \\(\\text{pH}\\) 9.3 best brackets the endpoint."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{pH}\\approx 7.66\\); the dianion hydrolyzes using \\(K_b=K_w/K_{a1}\\) at \\(0.0333\\ \\text{M}\\), so an indicator changing near \\(\\text{pH}\\) 7.7 best brackets the endpoint."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{pH}\\approx 4.25\\); the amphiprotic species \\(\\text{HA}^-\\) governs the endpoint, so \\(\\text{pH}=\\tfrac{1}{2}(\\text{p}K_{a1}+\\text{p}K_{a2})\\) and an indicator changing near \\(\\text{pH}\\) 4.3 best brackets it."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because reaching the second equivalence point of a diprotic acid requires two moles of base per mole of acid, and the species present there is the fully deprotonated malonate dianion, which must then be treated as a weak base. Step 1: the initial acid is \(0.02500\ \text{L}\times 0.100\ \text{M}=2.50\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\). Step 2: the second equivalence point needs \(2\times 2.50\times10^{-3}=5.00\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\ \text{NaOH}\), which is \(5.00\times10^{-3}/0.100=0.05000\ \text{L}=50.00\ \text{mL}\) of titrant, so the total volume is \(25.00+50.00=75.00\ \text{mL}=0.07500\ \text{L}\). Step 3: the dianion concentration is the limiting intermediate result, \(2.50\times10^{-3}/0.07500=0.0333\ \text{M}\) — the original concentration diluted by the added titrant. Step 4: the dianion is the conjugate base of \(\text{HA}^-\), whose acidity is set by \(\text{p}K_{a2}=5.7\), so \(K_b=K_w/K_{a2}=1.0\times10^{-14}/10^{-5.7}=5.0\times10^{-9}\). Step 5: \([\text{OH}^-]=\sqrt{K_b\,C}=\sqrt{(5.0\times10^{-9})(0.0333)}=1.3\times10^{-5}\ \text{M}\), giving \(\text{pOH}=4.89\) and \(\text{pH}=14.00-4.89=9.11\); an indicator with a transition near \(\text{pH}\) 9 therefore brackets this endpoint. (Choice B) This drops the dilution step: it correctly identifies the dianion as the weak base and uses \(K_{a2}\), but applies the original \(0.100\ \text{M}\) instead of the \(0.0333\ \text{M}\) that results after \(50.00\ \text{mL}\) of titrant has been added, inflating \([\text{OH}^-]\) and raising the computed \(\text{pH}\) to \(9.35\). (Choice C) This selects the wrong dissociation constant: the malonate dianion is the conjugate base of \(\text{HA}^-\), so its \(K_b\) must come from \(K_{a2}\), not from \(K_{a1}\); using \(K_b=K_w/K_{a1}\) understates the basicity and yields \(7.66\). (Choice D) This stops one equivalence point too early: the half-formula \(\text{pH}=\tfrac{1}{2}(\text{p}K_{a1}+\text{p}K_{a2})\) describes the amphiprotic \(\text{HA}^-\) present at the first equivalence point, not the dianion present at the second, so it gives \(4.25\) rather than the basic \(\text{pH}\) expected when a diprotic acid is fully neutralized by a strong base. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must chain a stoichiometric volume calculation, a dilution, and a weak-base equilibrium to arrive at the endpoint \(\text{pH}\).$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'fd43f4f0-3620-4c15-84e8-0595648ab5df';
